# Browser Automation

**Applies to**: Any task requiring browser interaction, UI inspection, screenshot capture, or console error checking.

## Tool Selection by Environment

| Environment | Correct tool | Wrong |
|-------------|-------------|-------|
| Cursor IDE | `cursor-ide-browser` MCP tools directly (`browser_navigate`, `browser_snapshot`, `browser_console_messages`, etc.) | `browser-use` subagent (Task tool) |
| Terminal (claude, claude-code, etc.) | `puppeteer-core` script (`puppeteer-core` is in `frontend/devDependencies`) | Installing full `puppeteer` package |
| Formal E2E test phase (`@ui-tester` agent) | `chrome-devtools` MCP | Either of the above |

## Rules

- **Never use the `browser-use` subagent** (Task tool with `subagent_type: "browser-use"`). It installs the full `puppeteer` package into `frontend/package.json` and leaves screenshot/script artefacts behind.
- **Never run `yarn add puppeteer`**. The project uses `puppeteer-core` (already installed). Use that.
- When `cursor-ide-browser` MCP tools are available, call them directly — do not delegate to a subagent.
- When writing a puppeteer script in terminal, use `puppeteer-core` and connect to an existing Chrome instance or launch via `executablePath`.

## Example (terminal — puppeteer-core)

```typescript
import puppeteer from 'puppeteer-core';

const browser = await puppeteer.launch({
  executablePath: '/Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome',
  headless: true,
});
const page = await browser.newPage();
await page.goto('http://localhost:3000');
const screenshot = await page.screenshot({ path: 'screenshot.png' });
await browser.close();
```

Run with: `yarn dlx tsx script.ts` (never `npx`)

## Why

The `browser-use` subagent adds `puppeteer` to `devDependencies` on every run, pollutes `yarn.lock`, and leaves artefacts in the repo. `cursor-ide-browser` MCP and `puppeteer-core` give the same capability without side effects.
